html: CJK text in a generic font, and justified CJK lines

A font-family that doesn't resolve (Chinese EPUB tools name "zw", a font
only e-readers have) falls back to the Latin serif font. Han chars then
fall back to a CJK font one by one, but the punctuation inside Chinese
text (“” …… ——) comes from the Latin font, half width. A CJK run in a
generic family (serif, sans-serif, monospace, flagged html_generic on
the font, whether builtin or a system font) now uses the script's
system font for the whole run, as browsers do.

Justified lines were only spread at spaces. Chinese has none, so its
lines ended ragged. The leftover space now also goes to the soft breaks
between CJK chars. (SumatraPDF #6500)

